pkgexec (0.0.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Pre-redemption results now echo the request correlation_id. Every effector
    return BEFORE receipt redemption (a lock miss -> apt_locked, an unsupported
    subset/targets, a resolve failure, a pre-redeem internal error, a
    half-installed refusal) and every redeem refusal issues no effect and left
    out_cid empty, so through the runix effect-session the result frame was
    discarded (correlation_id != the open-intent cid) and misclassified as an
    effect-unknown left-open instead of its true status. A contended apt.update was
    reported as "don't know if it ran" rather than the retryable apt_locked. Each
    effector now calls pkgx_cid_echo(out_cid, expected_cid) up front, so a
    no-effect result carries the already-open request cid and runix trusts it;
    redeem still overwrites out_cid on redeem_ok, so post-redemption paths keep the
    broker-validated cid and a genuinely-unknown or post-redemption failure is
    never given a fabricated cid. (Surfaced by the disposable-VM G10 gate once the
    runix empty-resource fix let apt.update reach the helper under contention.)

 -- Troy Hernandez <troy@cornball.ai>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:22:29 -0500

pkgexec (0.0.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * runix-apt-preview: a new unprivileged, read-only planner installed on PATH
    (/usr/bin). It reads the receipt-free stdin request {schema_version, verb,
    packages}, opens the apt cache without the dpkg lock, and prints one strict
    JSON object with the schema-1 resource and plan_hash. It is the single
    implementation of the preview descriptor/digest the pkgops issuer previews
    against, sharing the effectors' apt_common descriptor builders, policy, and
    digest; it commits nothing (no lock, no dpkg, no fetch, no shell, no receipt).
  * signed-by inline-armored-key normalization: an inline Signed-By PGP key carries
    '=' and newlines the schema-1 field grammar forbids, so apt.update failed closed
    on such a source (e.g. one shipping an embedded key). The shared source
    enumeration now maps a valid inline armored key to inline-sha256:<hex> over its
    exact bytes, binding the source's signing-key identity within the grammar.
    Path/fingerprint values are unchanged, so existing update hashes and plan
    schema 1 are preserved.
  * Checkpoint 1 of the planner slice extracted shared read-only descriptor builders
    for update/hold/configure into apt_common; the effectors are byte-identical.

 -- Troy Hernandez <troy@cornball.ai>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0000

pkgexec (0.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Activation: the nine per-verb pkexec/polkit entrypoints
    (install/remove/purge/upgrade/dist-upgrade/update/hold/unhold/configure) redeem
    a broker effect receipt over the authenticated transport and commit the apt
    transaction behind the redeem gate, each linking only its own mechanism. The
    committing verbs hand the dpkg locks to their spawned child (frontend/inner
    split) and isolate the strict JSON result channel from commit output.
  * Proven on a disposable systemd/polkit/dpkg VM (runix libapt-pkg-helper-plan.md
    §7): 23/23 polkit matrix, 37/37 §7 acceptance gates, rc=0.

 -- Troy Hernandez <troy@cornball.ai>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

pkgexec (0.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Slice 1 scaffold: schema-1 plan-digest encoder (OpenSSL EVP), strict stdin
    request parsing, and process-hygiene primitives, with sanitized tests and a
    read-only libapt-pkg API probe. No mutation-capable entrypoint.

